Developing an algorithm to identify the best-performing mutual fund based on various criteria can be a complex task, but it can be achieved using a combination of statistical analysis, data processing, and machine learning techniques. Here is a sample code in C++ that can help you achieve this:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
struct MutualFund {
    string name;
    double annualized_return;
    double yield;
    double expense_ratio;
    double turnover_rate;
    int experience;
};
double calculate_score(MutualFund mf) {
    double score = 0.0;
    score += mf.annualized_return;
    score += mf.yield >= 5.0 ? 1.0 : 0.0;
    score -= mf.expense_ratio;
    score -= mf.turnover_rate / 100.0;
    score += mf.experience / 10.0;
    return score;
}
bool compare_score(MutualFund mf1, MutualFund mf2) {
    return calculate_score(mf1) > calculate_score(mf2);
}
int main() {
    vector<MutualFund> mutual_funds = {
        {"Fund A", 0.08, 5.2, 0.75, 15, 5},
        {"Fund B", 0.09, 4.8, 0.5, 10, 7},
        {"Fund C", 0.1, 6.0, 0.9, 20, 3},
        {"Fund D", 0.07, 5.5, 0.6, 12, 9},
        {"Fund E", 0.11, 4.5, 0.4, 8, 6}
    };
    sort(mutual_funds.begin(), mutual_funds.end(), compare_score);
    cout << "The best mutual fund based on your criteria is: " << mutual_funds[0].name << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we define a `MutualFund` struct that contains the relevant data for each fund. We also define a `calculate_score` function that calculates a score for each mutual fund based on the given criteria. The `compare_score` function is used to sort the mutual funds based on their scores.
In the `main` function, we create a vector of mutual funds and sort them using the `compare_score` function. Finally, we print the name of the best mutual fund based on the given criteria.
