Here's a C++ program that can reverse a string while taking into consideration different scenarios such as strings with special characters, spaces, and punctuation marks. The program is optimized for efficiency and able to handle long strings with minimal overhead.
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
void reverseString(string& str) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = str.length() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (!isalnum(str[left])) {
            left++;
        }
        else if (!isalnum(str[right])) {
            right--;
        }
        else {
            swap(str[left], str[right]);
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
int main() {
    string str;
    cout << "Enter a string: ";
    getline(cin, str);
    reverseString(str);
    cout << "Reversed string: " << str << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
The `reverseString` function takes a reference to a string and reverses it by swapping characters from the beginning and end of the string until they meet in the middle. The function also checks for special characters, spaces, and punctuation marks by using the `isalnum` function to determine if a character is alphanumeric or not. If a character is not alphanumeric, the function skips over it and moves on to the next character.
The `main` function prompts the user to enter a string, calls the `reverseString` function to reverse the string, and then outputs the reversed string to the console.
Overall, this program is optimized for efficiency by using pointers to traverse the string and minimizing the number of operations required to reverse the string. It can handle long strings with minimal overhead since it only swaps characters as needed and does not create any additional copies of the string.

