One way to approach this problem is to use regular expressions to match the repeating pattern and then filter out the patterns that don't contain the desired character set. Here's an example code snippet in C# that demonstrates this approach:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        string input = "ABCABCABCABCABC";
        string pattern = @"(\w{3})\1+"; // matches any three-letter word repeated one or more times
        string vowels = "AEIOU";
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(input, pattern);
        foreach (Match match in matches) {
            string patternStr = match.Groups[1].Value;
            if (ContainsChars(patternStr, vowels)) {
                Console.WriteLine("Found pattern: " + patternStr);
            }
        }
    }
    static bool ContainsChars(string str, string charSet) {
        foreach (char c in charSet) {
            if (!str.Contains(c)) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
In this example, we first define the input string and the pattern we want to match using a regular expression. The pattern `(\w{3})\1+` matches any three-letter word repeated one or more times. The parentheses capture the three-letter word as a group, which we can later extract using `match.Groups[1].Value`.
We then define the set of vowels we want to filter for (in this case, "AEIOU").
We use `Regex.Matches` to find all occurrences of the pattern in the input string. We then iterate over the matches and extract the captured group using `match.Groups[1].Value`. We use the `ContainsChars` method to check if the pattern contains all the characters in the desired character set. If it does, we print out the pattern.
The `ContainsChars` method simply iterates over each character in the character set and checks if it is present in the input string. If any character is missing, it returns `false`. If all characters are present, it returns `true`.
This approach should be efficient for most inputs, as it uses regular expressions for pattern matching and only iterates over each pattern once to check for the desired character set.

